# decision SwiftPM owns one Package dependency expression

The Swift adapter edits one `.package(url:..., revision:...)` expression in the top-level
`Package(dependencies:)` array. Every dependency uses the full `LockedDependency.Commit` as
`revision`; requested branch names are never written. The export name identifies status output;
SwiftPM derives package identity from the Git URL. `Pull` runs `swift package resolve`, including
when the declaration is unchanged, so the exact revision is resolved and missing checkouts are
repaired. `Remove` removes only the owned root expression and resolves again, retaining unrelated
and transitively required packages.
SwiftPM cannot address a package below the repository root; `Capability` reports that source
shape as typed not-wirable so initial selection can use the explicit submodule fallback.
Only a `dependencies:` argument at the outer `Package(...)` call depth is eligible; target
dependency arrays with the same label are never edited. Insertions are delimited and remember
an added separator, empty array, or newly created top-level argument so unwire restores the
original Swift source bytes. A managed insertion into a non-empty single-line dependency array
uses an explicit inline marker so the closing bracket cannot be swallowed by a line comment and
removal still restores the original layout.

# invariant Source changes remove the old Swift identity

When a dependency source changes, the transaction unwires adapters using the old source before
wiring the new source. This prevents two Swift package identities from remaining in
`Package.swift`, and remains atomic with every other ecosystem.

# test Swift adapter

- Fixture/golden wiring is idempotent and preserves products, targets, comments, and unrelated
  package dependencies.
- Drift checks the normalized URL and locked revision.
- Unwire and source moves remove the old package expression only.

# decision SwiftPM lifecycle prerequisite

`Pull` and `Remove` check `swift` through the shared prerequisite contract before invoking it.
`Inspect` is offline and requires the pin whose location matches the dependency Git source in
`Package.resolved` to carry the exact locked revision. Another pin is not evidence.
